{
  "abstract": "A Lean 4 and Mathlib proof of the Mirsky–Newman theorem in two forms, both stated word for word as in google-deepmind/formal-conjectures (FC) at commit ea8e3e198ded6fc73efb7f5653b74b28ac4ae1a8. The first form (Erdős problem 947) says that no finite family of residue classes with pairwise distinct moduli n_i ≥ 2 partitions the integers, so there is no exact covering system with distinct moduli. The second form (a variant of Erdős problem 1190) says that if residue classes a_n mod n, for n in a finite set S of integers all greater than m ≥ 1, are pairwise disjoint, then the sum of 1/n over S is less than 1. Both are derived from one finite roots-of-unity lemma: pairwise disjoint residue classes with distinct moduli ≥ 2, all dividing N > 0, cannot cover {0, …, N−1}. The lemma is proved by summing ζ^x over {0, …, N−1}, where ζ is a primitive root of unity of order equal to the largest modulus. This entry gives an Apache-2.0-licensed, Comparator-checked proof of both FC statements; at the pinned commit FC links no formal proof of the second.",
